{"id":"fact-qn-001","type":"decision","fact":"JJRS final-selection fallback (after the while loop in qn_refinement_kernel) is correct for arbitrarily large nR-nL, not just the normal case nR-nL <= n. When the convergence guard triggers (sumP >= nR or sumQ <= nL), nR-nL can be up to n(n-1)/2. The fallback allocates 'new double[nR-nL]', fills all surviving pairs, then runs floyd_rivest_select or pdqselect. At n=50 this is 1225 pairs (~10 KB). The selection index k_off = k_target - nL - 1 remains valid since k_target > nL is a JJRS invariant.","recommendation":"Do not add a special-case allocation cap to the fallback. The existing code is already correct for degenerate inputs; the convergence guard is sufficient to prevent the infinite loop.","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"agent","reference":"qn-opt WU-Q8 JJRS convergence guard design verification","date":"2026-03-23"}],"tags":["jjrs","qn","correctness","fallback","selection"],"affectedFiles":["src/qn_estimator.cpp"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-03-23T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-03-23T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
